  "image_name": "b0b23cfb2bdadd534c0a5026d8200db9.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how DNA hybridization is used as a programmable tool to build nanoscale structures and particle assemblies. At the top, two complementary single DNA strands pair through sequence matching to form a double helix, illustrating the basic reversible binding interaction that drives all of the later designs.\n\nUsing this principle, DNA can act as a structural “brick” or as part of DNA origami. In the brick concept, short DNA motifs with designed connections fit together like modular building blocks and can be rotated and stacked into larger ordered 3D lattices. In DNA origami, many DNA helices are arranged side by side and connected by crossover strands to create defined 2D and 3D architectures with nanometer-scale control.\n\nThe lower panels show DNA attached to colloidal or nanoparticle surfaces. Complementary DNA linkers bridge different particles, causing them to aggregate, while heating and cooling reversibly switch assembly and disassembly. Finally, DNA templates can organize nanoparticles into molecule-like clusters with prescribed geometry. Overall, the image emphasizes DNA not just as genetic material, but as a highly programmable construction material for assembling complex nanostructures and responsive particle systems.",
